{"text": "Cameron J. Cox\nState Bar Number 24035802\ncameron@attydenton.com\n(940) 390-7145\n\nTexas Tech University School of Law\nLubbock, Texas\nJuris Doctor, 2005\n\nTexas Tech University\nLubbock, Texas\nB.B.A. Economics, Minor Political Science, 2001\ne Graduated with Honors in Economics\ne Graduated from the Honors College\ne Graduated in 3.5 years in the top 10% of class\n\nTIME TO SELL BASTROP\n\n| absolutely love Bastrop and would cherish the opportunity to sell Bastrop all over the state and U.S. |am not just an experienced\nlitigator who successfully served as lead counsel on over 262 lawsuits. A vast majority of those lawsuits involved property issues,\nnon-profit governance, business, contract, and consumer law matters- all matters relevant to leading the Bastrop EDC. | also\nserved in leadership roles for over a dozen non-profits, commissions, and committees in the city of Denton and Denton County\nfor over a decade. | oversaw and led two strategic business plans for both the Denton Chamber of Commerce and the Children\u2019s\nAdvocacy Center for Denton County. Both those plans took about a year to accomplish and included budgeting for growth,\nfacility construction, developing a brand, developing new websites, coordinating fundraising events, and increasing community\nawareness/participation and donor base. | also oversaw the budgets of many of these organizations through those roles as\nchair, president, and/or board of director. These budgets are comparable size to the Bastrop Economic Development\nCorporation\u2019s budget. | did all this work for these organizations as a volunteer during my free-time. Imagine what | can\naccomplish for the Bastrop EDC as a full-time paid director. Time to put me to work on our strategic plan and future of Bastrop\nand Bastrop EDC.\n\nPROFESSIONAL WORK HISTORY\n\nSawko & Burroughs, LLP\nAssociate Attorney, 2005-2011\n\nstarted my career at the right place. | began my legal career in 2005 as an associate attorney with the Mayor of our town,\nMark Burroughs. It was here | developed my sense of Servant Leadership. My very first lawsuit was filed on behalf of a non-\nprofit, the Cumberland Presbyterian Children\u2019s Home. | volunteered my services for free to help them obtain a parcel of\nand. This land was ultimately sold to pay for one of their cottages that housed up to ten homeless and/or abandoned\nchildren. | also began my business litigation, property law, non-profit, and tax legal work at this firm. For the next six years,\ngrew as a person, lawyer, and Christian helping others professionally and personally.\n\nElsey & Elsey\nunior Partner, 2011-2014\n\ndecided it was time to expand my areas of practice, so | moved to Elsey & Elsey in 2011 to learn family law from one of the\nbest in our area, Chad Elsey. The Elsey family also has a service-over-self-mentality, which is another reason | joined with\nhem. In return, | helped the Elseys establish, develop, and then manage their own property tax collection business and\nstaff, and grew that business into four-employee $300-400k a year side venture for the firm. This success is the reason they\nmade me partner of their father-son team, which as an honor.",
